          </section>

          <!-- Prerequisites -->
          <section class="recipe-section" id="api-prerequisites">
            <h3 class="recipe-section__heading">Prerequisites</h3>
            <ul class="recipe-prereq">
              <li>Node.js 22.5 or later, Git, an AI coding assistant and an agent runtime for the convoy — as in the <a href="#prerequisites">first recipe</a></li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <!-- Step 1 -->
          <section class="recipe-section recipe-step" id="api-step-1">
            <div class="recipe-step__number">1</div>
            <h3 class="recipe-section__heading">Step 1: Initialize</h3>
            <p class="recipe-section__body">Create a new directory, initialize git and npm, run the OpenCastle initializer, and commit what it wrote. In an empty project it finds no stack — the output is the same as in the <a href="#step-1">first recipe</a>, and every flag is on the <a href="/docs/cli#init">CLI page</a>.</p>
            <div class="recipe-code">
              <div class="recipe-code__header">Terminal</div>
              <pre><code>$ mkdir task-api
$ cd task-api
$ git init
$ npm init -y
$ npx opencastle init
$ git add -A
$ git commit -m "Add OpenCastle"</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="recipe-output">
              <pre><code>  …
  Integrations detected:
    chrome-devtools

  Set this up? [Y/n]
  …
  ✓ Created 113 files
  …</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="recipe-tip"><strong>Tip:</strong> Name the server framework in your description in Step 2 — Express, Fastify or another. OpenCastle has no integration for either, so there is nothing to add here.</div>
          </section>

          <!-- Step 2 -->
          <section class="recipe-section recipe-step" id="api-step-2">
            <div class="recipe-step__number">2</div>
            <h3 class="recipe-section__heading">Step 2: Describe the API</h3>
            <p class="recipe-section__body">Run <code>npx opencastle convoy "&lt;task&gt;"</code> with a description of the API. It generates a PRD, validates it, assesses complexity, and produces a convoy spec — the full run is shown in the <a href="#step-2">first recipe</a>. Answer <strong>n</strong> when it asks <code>Run it? [Y/n]</code>: you'll review the PRD first.</p>
            <div class="recipe-code">
              <div class="recipe-code__header">Terminal</div>
              <pre><code>$ npx opencastle convoy "Build a REST API for a task management app \
  with users, projects, and tasks, using Express. Include JWT authentication, \
  role-based access control, input validation with Zod, and comprehensive test coverage."</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="recipe-output">
              <pre><code>  …
  Pipeline complete!

  PRD:           .opencastle/prds/rest-api-task-management.prd.md
  Convoy spec:   .opencastle/convoys/rest-api-task-management.convoy.yml
  …
  Run it? [Y/n]</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="recipe-tip"><strong>Tip:</strong> Keep the prompt focused and include explicit constraints (e.g., "use Zod", "JWT", "SQLite") so generated code matches your stack.</div>
          </section>

          <!-- Step 3 -->
          <section class="recipe-section recipe-step" id="api-step-3">
            <div class="recipe-step__number">3</div>
            <h3 class="recipe-section__heading">Step 3: Review the PRD</h3>
            <p class="recipe-section__body">OpenCastle writes the PRD to <code>.opencastle/prds/</code>. Review and tweak endpoints, auth rules, and validation before running the convoy.</p>
            <div class="recipe-code">
              <div class="recipe-code__header">.opencastle/prds/rest-api-task-management.prd.md (excerpt)</div>
              <pre><code># REST API Task Management — PRD

## Technical Requirements
| Method | Path | Auth | Description |
|--------|------|------|-------------|
| GET | /api/v1/users | admin, user | list users |
| POST | /api/v1/users | admin | create user |
| GET | /api/v1/users/:id | admin, user | get user |
| PUT | /api/v1/users/:id | admin | update user |
| DELETE | /api/v1/users/:id | admin | delete user |
| GET | /api/v1/projects | admin, user | list projects |
| POST | /api/v1/projects | admin | create project |
| GET | /api/v1/projects/:id | admin, user | get project |
| PUT | /api/v1/projects/:id | admin | update project |
| DELETE | /api/v1/projects/:id | admin | delete project |
| GET | /api/v1/tasks | admin, user | list tasks |
| POST | /api/v1/tasks | admin, user | create task |
| GET | /api/v1/tasks/:id | admin, user | get task |
| PUT | /api/v1/tasks/:id | admin, user | update task |
| DELETE | /api/v1/tasks/:id | admin, user | delete task |

- JWT in `Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;`; roles: admin, user; role checks per route
- Users: `email` (email), `password` (min 8)
- Projects: `name` (string, required)
- Tasks: `title` (string, required), `status` (enum: open|in_progress|done), `assigneeId` (uuid)
…</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="recipe-tip"><strong>Tip:</strong> After editing the PRD, rebuild the spec from it: <code>npx opencastle convoy plan --prd .opencastle/prds/rest-api-task-management.prd.md</code>. It reuses the complexity assessment saved beside the PRD, so delete <code>rest-api-task-management.complexity.json</code> first if you changed the scope.</div>
          </section>

          <!-- Step 4 -->
          <section class="recipe-section recipe-step" id="api-step-4">
            <div class="recipe-step__number">4</div>
            <h3 class="recipe-section__heading">Step 4: Preview the plan</h3>
            <p class="recipe-section__body">Preview the convoy execution plan with <code>--dry-run</code> on the <code>run</code> command. This shows the dependency graph and execution order without starting an agent.</p>
            <div class="recipe-code">
              <div class="recipe-code__header">Terminal</div>
              <pre><code>$ npx opencastle convoy run .opencastle/convoys/rest-api-task-management.convoy.yml --dry-run</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="recipe-output">
              <pre><code>  …
  🏰 Execution Plan: REST API task management
  Adapter:     copilot
  Concurrency: 3
  On failure:  stop
  Tasks:       8
  ──────────────────────────────────
  Phase 1:
    setup-project — Scaffold Express with TypeScript, Zod and Vitest [developer, 30m] [package.json, tsconfig.json, src/app.ts, src/server.ts]

  Phase 2:
    db-schema — Create SQLite schema, migrations and seeds [data-engineer, 30m] (after: setup-project) [src/db/]
    auth-middleware — Add JWT authentication and role checks [security-expert, 30m] (after: setup-project) [src/middleware/auth.ts]

  Phase 3:
    user-routes — CRUD routes for users [developer, 30m] (after: db-schema, auth-middleware) [src/routes/users.ts, src/schemas/user.ts]
    project-routes — CRUD routes for projects [developer, 30m] (after: db-schema, auth-middleware) [src/routes/projects.ts, src/schemas/project.ts]
    task-routes — CRUD routes for tasks [developer, 30m] (after: db-schema, auth-middleware) [src/routes/tasks.ts, src/schemas/task.ts]

  Phase 4:
    route-index — Mount routes behind the auth middleware [developer, 30m] (after: user-routes, project-routes, task-routes) [src/routes/index.ts]

  Phase 5:
    integration-tests — Integration tests for every endpoint [testing-expert, 30m] (after: route-index) [tests/]</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="recipe-tip"><strong>Tip:</strong> Use the dry-run to confirm the decomposition and catch unwanted changes before executing.</div>
          </section>

          <!-- Step 5 -->
          <section class="recipe-section recipe-step" id="api-step-5">
            <div class="recipe-step__number">5</div>
            <h3 class="recipe-section__heading">Step 5: Execute</h3>
            <p class="recipe-section__body">Run the convoy to execute the plan. Each task runs in an isolated git worktree and is merged into <code>feat/rest-api-task-management</code> when it passes review; the <a href="#step-5">first recipe</a> describes a run.</p>
            <div class="recipe-code">
              <div class="recipe-code__header">Terminal</div>
              <pre><code>$ npx opencastle convoy run .opencastle/convoys/rest-api-task-management.convoy.yml</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="recipe-tip"><strong>Tip:</strong> A failed task is retried once by default (<code>max_retries</code> in the spec). To continue after an interruption or a failure, use <code>npx opencastle convoy resume</code>; it re-runs only what is not done.</div>
          </section>

          <!-- Step 6 -->
          <section class="recipe-section recipe-step" id="api-step-6">
            <div class="recipe-step__number">6</div>
            <h3 class="recipe-section__heading">Step 6: Test the API</h3>
            <p class="recipe-section__body">Check out the convoy's branch, run the generated test suite and start the server — the scripts are whatever the setup task wrote to <code>package.json</code>.</p>
            <div class="recipe-code">
              <div class="recipe-code__header">Terminal</div>
              <pre><code>$ git checkout feat/rest-api-task-management
$ npm test
$ npm start</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="recipe-section__body">Then exercise an endpoint. With an empty database and a valid token, the task list returns an empty <code>data</code> array and a <code>meta.total</code> of 0.</p>
            <div class="recipe-code">
              <div class="recipe-code__header">Terminal</div>
              <pre><code>$ curl -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" http://localhost:3000/api/v1/tasks | jq</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="recipe-tip"><strong>Tip:</strong> If a test fails, the run viewer (<code>npx opencastle convoy dashboard</code>) shows which task changed which files, with its gate results and log.</div>
          </section>

          <!-- Step 7 -->
          <section class="recipe-section recipe-step" id="api-step-7">
            <div class="recipe-step__number">7</div>
            <h3 class="recipe-section__heading">Step 7: Review &amp; iterate</h3>
            <p class="recipe-section__body">Review the generated code and use your assistant to request tweaks. Then push the branch and open a PR.</p>
            <div class="recipe-code">
              <div class="recipe-code__header">Terminal</div>
              <pre><code>$ git push -u origin feat/rest-api-task-management
$ gh pr create --title "feat: REST API for task management" --body "Built with OpenCastle's convoy engine"</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="recipe-tip"><strong>Tip:</strong> To act on review comments, use the <code>/oc:resolve-pr-comments</code> prompt; for small tweaks, <code>/oc:quick-refinement</code>. <a href="/docs/prompts#how-to-use">How to run a prompt</a> in your assistant.</div>
          </section>

          <!-- Tips & Troubleshooting -->
          <section class="recipe-section" id="api-troubleshooting">
            <h3 class="recipe-section__heading">Tips &amp; Troubleshooting</h3>
            <div class="recipe-troubleshooting">
              <div class="recipe-troubleshooting__item">
                <p class="recipe-troubleshooting__problem">Port 3000 already in use</p>
                <p class="recipe-troubleshooting__solution">Change the PORT environment variable before starting the server: <code>PORT=4000 npm start</code>.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="recipe-troubleshooting__item">
                <p class="recipe-troubleshooting__problem">Authentication middleware not applied</p>
                <p class="recipe-troubleshooting__solution">Check that the auth middleware is mounted before the protected routes in <code>src/routes/index.ts</code>. To have the Team Lead trace it, run the <code>/oc:bug-fix</code> prompt with the request that got through.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="recipe-troubleshooting__item">
                <p class="recipe-troubleshooting__problem">Tests failing on CI</p>
                <p class="recipe-troubleshooting__solution">Ensure CI provides required env vars (JWT secret, DB URL) and run migrations in the CI job before tests.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="recipe-troubleshooting__item">
                <p class="recipe-troubleshooting__problem">Want to add more endpoints later</p>
                <p class="recipe-troubleshooting__solution">Run <code>npx opencastle convoy "&lt;task&gt;"</code> with a description of the new endpoints. This generates a new PRD and convoy spec for the additions. For quick changes, use the <code>/oc:quick-refinement</code> prompt.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- What's next -->
          <section class="recipe-section" id="api-whats-next">
            <h3 class="recipe-section__heading">What's next</h3>
            <ul class="recipe-section__list">
              <li><strong><a href="/docs/cli">CLI Reference</a></strong> — full command options and flags</li>
              <li><strong><a href="/docs/agentic-development">Agentic Development</a></strong> — guide to working with agent teams and PR workflows</li>
              <li><strong><a href="#team-work">Team Work</a></strong> — recipe for multi-agent collaboration on larger features</li>
              <li><strong><a href="#existing-project">Existing Project</a></strong> — how to onboard OpenCastle into a repo with existing code</li>
            </ul>
