        </article>

        <article class="recipe" id="existing-project">
          <h2 class="recipe__title">
            <svg width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"/><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2"/><line x1="8" y1="7" x2="16" y2="7"/><line x1="8" y1="11" x2="13" y2="11"/></svg>
            Add OpenCastle to an Existing Project
          </h2>

          <!-- Introduction -->
          <section class="recipe-section" id="existing-intro">
            <h3 class="recipe-section__heading">Introduction</h3>
            <p class="recipe-section__body">This recipe adds OpenCastle to a repository that already has code, tests, CI, and established conventions. <code>opencastle init</code> detects your stack and asks once. It doesn't touch your source code. Instruction files you wrote at the root — <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, <code>AGENTS.md</code>, <code>.cursorrules</code>, <code>.github/copilot-instructions.md</code> — keep your content above a managed block. A file of yours inside a directory OpenCastle generates, such as <code>.cursor/rules/</code> or <code>.github/agents/</code>, is named by init and removed by the next <code>sync</code>; move it into <code>.opencastle/</code> and it is compiled for every assistant.</p>
          </section>

          <!-- What you'll set up -->
          <section class="recipe-section" id="existing-what-youll-set-up">
            <h3 class="recipe-section__heading">What you'll set up</h3>
            <p class="recipe-section__body">A fully configured OpenCastle workspace layered on top of your existing project, with the integrations init detected and a first feature built through the convoy.</p>
            <ul class="recipe-section__list">
              <li><strong>Initialize</strong> — detect the stack and add OpenCastle config without touching code</li>
              <li><strong>Review integrations</strong> — check what init detected in the manifest</li>
              <li><strong>PRD</strong> — create a first PRD for a new feature in the existing codebase</li>
              <li><strong>Execute</strong> — run convoy gated on existing tests and lint</li>
              <li><strong>Verify</strong> — review, iterate, and merge through your normal workflow</li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <!-- Prerequisites -->
          <section class="recipe-section" id="existing-prerequisites">
            <h3 class="recipe-section__heading">Prerequisites</h3>
            <ul class="recipe-prereq">
              <li>An existing project with git history and at least one commit</li>
              <li>Node.js 22.5 or later, an AI coding assistant and an agent runtime for the convoy — as in the <a href="#prerequisites">first recipe</a></li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <!-- Step 1 -->
          <section class="recipe-section recipe-step" id="existing-step-1">
            <div class="recipe-step__number">1</div>
            <h3 class="recipe-section__heading">Step 1: Initialize OpenCastle</h3>
            <p class="recipe-section__body">Run <code>npx opencastle init</code> from the project root. It reads <code>package.json</code>, <code>tsconfig.json</code> and other config files, shows the integrations it found, and asks once. Here, a Next.js 15 project with TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, Vitest and ESLint:</p>
            <div class="recipe-code">
              <div class="recipe-code__header">Terminal</div>
              <pre><code>$ npx opencastle init</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="recipe-output">
              <pre><code>  …
  Integrations detected:
    nextjs, vitest, chrome-devtools

  Set this up? [Y/n]
  Configuring project...
  ✓ Populated 3 config files
  → Removed 8 unused template(s)
  ✓ Created 115 files
  …</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="recipe-section__body">The full output is in the <a href="#step-1">first recipe</a>, and every flag is on the <a href="/docs/cli#init">CLI page</a>. Commit what init wrote on a branch of its own — the convoy in Step 5 runs each task in a git worktree made from your last commit, so the config has to be committed first.</p>
            <div class="recipe-code">
              <div class="recipe-code__header">Terminal</div>
              <pre><code>$ git checkout -b chore/opencastle-config
$ git add .gitignore .opencastle .github .vscode
$ git commit -m "chore: add OpenCastle configuration"</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="recipe-tip"><strong>Tip:</strong> Next, run the <a href="/docs/prompts#bootstrap-customizations">bootstrap-customizations</a> prompt once — <code>/oc:bootstrap-customizations</code> in Claude Code and Copilot Chat. It reads your schemas, routes and environment variables and fills in what init could not detect.</div>
          </section>

          <!-- Step 2 -->
          <section class="recipe-section recipe-step" id="existing-step-2">
            <div class="recipe-step__number">2</div>
            <h3 class="recipe-section__heading">Step 2: Review detected integrations</h3>
            <p class="recipe-section__body">OpenCastle stores your stack in <code>.opencastle/manifest.json</code>. <code>stack</code> lists the integrations it installed; <code>repoInfo</code> is everything it detected, including tools that have no integration of their own, like Tailwind CSS here.</p>
            <div class="recipe-code">
              <div class="recipe-code__header">.opencastle/manifest.json (excerpt)</div>
              <pre><code>{`{
  …
  "stack": {
    "ides": [
      "vscode"
    ],
    "techTools": [
      "nextjs",
      "vitest",
      "chrome-devtools"
    ],
    "teamTools": []
  },
  "repoInfo": {
    "language": "typescript",
    "mcpConfig": true,
    "frameworks": [
      "next"
    ],
    "testing": [
      "vitest",
      "chrome-devtools"
    ],
    "styling": [
      "tailwind"
    ],
    …
  }
}`}</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="recipe-tip"><strong>Tip:</strong> To add an integration init missed, run <code>npx opencastle add &lt;pack&gt;</code> (<code>npx opencastle add --list</code> shows them all). To pick everything by hand, run <code>npx opencastle init --customize</code>.</div>
          </section>

          <!-- Step 3 -->
          <section class="recipe-section recipe-step" id="existing-step-3">
            <div class="recipe-step__number">3</div>
            <h3 class="recipe-section__heading">Step 3: Verify setup with doctor</h3>
            <p class="recipe-section__body">Run <code>npx opencastle doctor</code> to verify the workspace is correctly configured. Every check it runs is listed on the <a href="/docs/cli#doctor">CLI page</a>.</p>
            <div class="recipe-code">
              <div class="recipe-code__header">Terminal</div>
              <pre><code>$ npx opencastle doctor</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="recipe-output">
              <pre><code>  …
  ✓ OpenCastle manifest (.opencastle/manifest.json)  v1.0.0, IDE: vscode
  ✓ Customizations directory  15 entries
  ! Skill matrix  11 unresolved capability slot(s)
  …
  All checks passed with 1 warning(s).</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="recipe-tip"><strong>Tip:</strong> A <code>!</code> is a warning, not a failure. Unresolved capability slots are parts of a stack — a CMS, payments, email — that this project has no integration for. Fix any <code>✗</code> before running convoys.</div>
          </section>

          <!-- Step 4 -->
          <section class="recipe-section recipe-step" id="existing-step-4">
            <div class="recipe-step__number">4</div>
            <h3 class="recipe-section__heading">Step 4: Generate a PRD for a new feature</h3>
            <p class="recipe-section__body">Describe a small, scoped feature. Let <code>npx opencastle convoy "&lt;task&gt;"</code> write the PRD and spec, or write <code>.opencastle/prds/&lt;name&gt;.prd.md</code> yourself and run <code>npx opencastle convoy plan --prd &lt;path&gt;</code>. Reference existing code paths so agents understand context. Answer <strong>n</strong> when it asks <code>Run it? [Y/n]</code>: you'll review the PRD first.</p>
            <div class="recipe-code">
              <div class="recipe-code__header">Terminal</div>
              <pre><code>$ npx opencastle convoy "Add user notifications with a server-side API \
  at /api/notifications and a UI inbox component. Include a database migration \
  for a notifications table and unit tests for both API and UI."</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="recipe-output">
              <pre><code>  …
  Pipeline complete!

  PRD:           .opencastle/prds/user-notifications.prd.md
  Convoy spec:   .opencastle/convoys/user-notifications.convoy.yml
  …
  Run it? [Y/n]</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="recipe-tip"><strong>Tip:</strong> Reference existing code paths (files and folders) so agents can locate relevant code quickly.</div>
          </section>

          <!-- Step 5 -->
          <section class="recipe-section recipe-step" id="existing-step-5">
            <div class="recipe-step__number">5</div>
            <h3 class="recipe-section__heading">Step 5: Review the PRD and run</h3>
            <p class="recipe-section__body">Review the generated PRD to confirm it captures the right scope. Tweak requirements, file paths, or constraints before running the convoy.</p>
            <div class="recipe-code">
              <div class="recipe-code__header">.opencastle/prds/user-notifications.prd.md (excerpt)</div>
              <pre><code># User Notifications — PRD

## Overview
Server-side notification API with a database migration and a UI inbox component.

## Implementation Scope
| Concern | Files / Directories |
|---------|---------------------|
| Database | `db/migrations/add_notifications.sql` |
| API | `app/api/notifications/route.ts`, `app/api/notifications/[id]/route.ts` |
| UI | `components/NotificationInbox/` |
| Tests | `__tests__/api/notifications.test.ts`, `__tests__/components/NotificationInbox.test.tsx` |
…</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="recipe-section__body">If you edit the PRD, rebuild the spec with <code>npx opencastle convoy plan --prd .opencastle/prds/user-notifications.prd.md</code>. Then preview the execution plan and run:</p>
            <div class="recipe-code">
              <div class="recipe-code__header">Terminal</div>
              <pre><code>$ npx opencastle convoy run .opencastle/convoys/user-notifications.convoy.yml --dry-run
$ npx opencastle convoy run .opencastle/convoys/user-notifications.convoy.yml</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="recipe-output">
              <pre><code>  …
  Phase 1:
    db-migration — Create notifications table migration [data-engineer, 30m] [db/migrations/add_notifications.sql]

  Phase 2:
    api-routes — Notification API: list, mark read, delete [developer, 30m] (after: db-migration) [app/api/notifications/route.ts, app/api/notifications/[id]/route.ts]
    notification-inbox — Inbox component with unread badge [ui-ux-expert, 30m] (after: db-migration) [components/NotificationInbox/]

  Phase 3:
    api-tests — Unit tests for the API handlers [testing-expert, 30m] (after: api-routes) [__tests__/api/notifications.test.ts]
    inbox-tests — Unit tests for the inbox component [testing-expert, 30m] (after: notification-inbox) [__tests__/components/NotificationInbox.test.tsx]</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="recipe-tip"><strong>Tip:</strong> Add lint, type-check, and test commands to the <code>gates</code> section of your convoy spec to catch regressions automatically. For example: <code>gates: ["npm run lint", "npx tsc --noEmit", "npm test"]</code></div>
          </section>

          <!-- Step 6 -->
          <section class="recipe-section recipe-step" id="existing-step-6">
            <div class="recipe-step__number">6</div>
            <h3 class="recipe-section__heading">Step 6: Verify compatibility</h3>
            <p class="recipe-section__body">Check out the feature branch the convoy built, inspect the changes, and run your existing test suite and linter to confirm nothing broke. Doctor's output is the same as in Step 3.</p>
            <div class="recipe-code">
              <div class="recipe-code__header">Terminal</div>
              <pre><code>$ git checkout feat/user-notifications
$ npm test
$ npm run lint
$ npx opencastle doctor</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="recipe-tip"><strong>Tip:</strong> Doctor checks your overall setup health. Run your project’s own test suite and linter separately to catch code-level regressions.</div>
          </section>

          <!-- Step 7 -->
          <section class="recipe-section recipe-step" id="existing-step-7">
            <div class="recipe-step__number">7</div>
            <h3 class="recipe-section__heading">Step 7: Iterate & expand</h3>
            <p class="recipe-section__body">Push both branches and open two pull requests: the configuration from Step 1, and the feature. The feature branch starts from the configuration commit, so merge the configuration first; until then the feature's pull request shows both. Use OpenCastle progressively for additional features — start small and increase scope as confidence grows.</p>
            <div class="recipe-code">
              <div class="recipe-code__header">Terminal</div>
              <pre><code>$ git push -u origin chore/opencastle-config feat/user-notifications
$ gh pr create --head chore/opencastle-config --title "chore: add OpenCastle configuration" --body "OpenCastle config for every assistant"
$ gh pr create --head feat/user-notifications --title "feat: user notifications" --body "Built with OpenCastle's convoy engine"</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="recipe-tip"><strong>Tip:</strong> Start with small, well-scoped features to build confidence before tackling larger refactors.</div>
          </section>

          <!-- Tips & Troubleshooting -->
          <section class="recipe-section" id="existing-troubleshooting">
            <h3 class="recipe-section__heading">Tips & Troubleshooting</h3>
            <div class="recipe-troubleshooting">
              <div class="recipe-troubleshooting__item">
                <p class="recipe-troubleshooting__problem">Init missed a framework</p>
                <p class="recipe-troubleshooting__solution">Add it with <code>npx opencastle add &lt;pack&gt;</code>, or run <code>npx opencastle init --customize</code> to pick everything by hand. Running plain <code>init</code> again asks <code>Re-initialize? [y/N]</code> and detects the same things.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="recipe-troubleshooting__item">
                <p class="recipe-troubleshooting__problem">init names files of yours</p>
                <p class="recipe-troubleshooting__solution">They sit in a directory OpenCastle generates, and the next <code>sync</code> removes them. Move each into <code>.opencastle/</code> — <code>instructions/</code>, <code>skills/&lt;name&gt;/</code> or <code>agents/</code> — and the next sync compiles it for every assistant.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="recipe-troubleshooting__item">
                <p class="recipe-troubleshooting__problem">Existing tests fail after convoy</p>
                <p class="recipe-troubleshooting__solution">Add lint, type-check, and test commands to the <code>gates</code> section of your convoy spec so they run at the end of every run. Inspect failing diffs and revert with git if required.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="recipe-troubleshooting__item">
                <p class="recipe-troubleshooting__problem">Want to use OpenCastle alongside existing CI</p>
                <p class="recipe-troubleshooting__solution">The convoy produces a standard git branch, and you open the pull request — your existing CI runs on it as usual. <code>npx opencastle ci</code> adds a check that the generated config matches its sources.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </section>

